          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient has anaemia with a low reticulocyte count. Which underlying mechanism </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient has anaemia with a low reticulocyte count. Which underlying mechanism does this finding indicate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Splenic sequestration of mature erythrocytes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hypersplenism destroys cells peripherally and triggers a compensatory reticulocyte response — the count would be high, not low.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Increased peripheral destruction of red blood cells</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Haemolysis stimulates the marrow to release more reticulocytes, producing a high reticulocyte count, not a low one.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Acute blood loss triggering a compensatory marrow response</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Acute blood loss, like haemolysis, triggers increased reticulocyte production. A low count rules out an adequate compensatory response.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inadequate red cell production by the bone marrow</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. A low reticulocyte count signals the marrow is failing to produce enough red cells — pointing to deficiency states (iron, B12, folate), marrow failure, or erythropoietin loss.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "A low reticulocyte count means the marrow is underproducing red cells. A high reticulocyte count points to haemolysis or blood loss where the marrow is compensating."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! The reticulocyte count is the key branch point: low = production problem (deficiency, marrow failure), high = destruction or blood loss."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
