          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Why must the differential leukocyte count be performed in the monolayer of the s</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Why must the differential leukocyte count be performed in the monolayer of the smear rather than at the feather edge?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The feather edge contains too few cells for an accurate count</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The feather edge does contain cells, but they are unevenly distributed. The problem is disproportionate accumulation of specific cell types, not overall cell scarcity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Neutrophils disproportionately accumulate at the feather edge, inflating their percentage by 10–15 points</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. During smear preparation, larger cells like neutrophils are pushed to the feather edge, creating a selection bias that falsely elevates their proportion in the differential.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Leishman stain does not penetrate the feather edge adequately</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Staining quality is generally adequate across the smear. The issue is uneven cellular distribution, not staining penetration.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Platelet clumps at the feather edge interfere with white blood cell identification</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Platelet clumping is a separate pre-analytical artefact (pseudothrombocytopenia) that affects the analyser count, not the reason for avoiding the feather edge during DLC.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "During smear preparation, larger cells — especially neutrophils — are dragged to the feather edge, inflating their proportion by 10–15%. The monolayer gives an accurate differential."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! The feather edge concentrates neutrophils, skewing the differential. Always count in the monolayer using a systematic battlements pattern."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
