          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient has microcytic anaemia (MCV 70 fL) with an RPI below 2. The peripheral</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient has microcytic anaemia (MCV 70 fL) with an RPI below 2. The peripheral smear shows hypochromic microcytic cells with target cells. Which pair of investigations would best distinguish iron deficiency anaemia from beta-thalassaemia trait?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Direct Coombs test and serum haptoglobin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>These investigate haemolytic anaemia. With an RPI below 2, the mechanism is underproduction, not peripheral destruction — haemolysis markers are irrelevant here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum ferritin and haemoglobin electrophoresis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Low ferritin confirms depleted iron stores (IDA), while haemoglobin electrophoresis detects raised HbA2 in beta-thalassaemia trait. Together they distinguish the two commonest causes of microcytic anaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum LDH and indirect bilirubin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>These are markers of red cell destruction (haemolysis). They do not help differentiate IDA from thalassaemia trait, which are both production-related microcytic anaemias.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Serum vitamin B12 and folate levels</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>B12 and folate deficiency cause macrocytic anaemia (MCV &gt; 100 fL). These tests are not indicated when the MCV is low.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "When MCV is low and RPI confirms underproduction, the main differentials are IDA and thalassaemia. Serum ferritin assesses iron stores; haemoglobin electrophoresis detects raised HbA2 in beta-thal trait."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! In microcytic anaemia with a low RPI, ferritin and haemoglobin electrophoresis are the targeted second-line tests that distinguish IDA from beta-thalassaemia trait."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
